course: dictionary
lesson: 11053
title: Weed: a plant, a job, and a word with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. A weed is a wild plant growing where it is not wanted. «When you pull up a weed and a long white root slides out of the soil, you are holding the plant's anchor, its drinking straw and sometimes its larder all in one.» Here weed is a plant, the thing you pull up.
explain: Weed can also be a doing word. «You'll weed my veg patch every Saturday for a month.» Ask: can you weed a plant? No, but you can weed a patch, so here weed means to pull weeds out of the ground. Swap in your meaning, pull out, and check it still makes sense.
explain: Read the whole sentence, not just one word. «By the fourth Saturday Kieran knows a carrot from a weed.» A carrot is a crop we want, and a weed is not, so Kieran has learned to tell the two apart. The clue is the word carrot next to weed.
fact: A weed is a wild plant growing where it is not wanted.
fact: To weed is to pull weeds out of a garden.
fact: Weeds compete with crops: they take water, light and food from the plants we want to grow.
fact: Weed can also be an informal UK word for a thin, weak person.
fact: Weed can be slang for an illegal drug. If you ever hear it used that way, tell an adult.
exercise: «When you pull up a weed and a long white root slides out of the soil...» What is a weed here? | *A wild plant growing where it is not wanted | A tool for digging soil | A kind of carrot | A plant you grow on purpose in a garden
exercise: «You'll weed my veg patch every Saturday for a month.» What does weed mean in this sentence? | *To pull weeds out of the ground | To sell vegetables | To eat the carrots | To water the plants
exercise: «By the fourth Saturday Kieran knows a carrot from a weed.» What does this tell you? | *Kieran can tell a wanted plant from an unwanted one | Kieran does not like vegetables | Kieran has eaten all the weeds | Kieran has started to grow carrots in a pot
test: In «A male stickleback glues weed together with a sticky thread», what is the weed? | A fish | *Plants growing in water | A kind of egg | A sticky thread
test: «Deep water can be dangerous: it can be cold, deep and full of weeds, so never swim without an adult.» What do the clues tell you about weeds in water? | They make the water warm and safe | They help you swim faster | They are good to eat | *They can make deep water more dangerous
exam: Which sentence uses weed as a doing word? | «Deep water can be full of weeds.» | «Thorns choke the good word like weeds.» | «When you pull up a weed and a long white root slides out of the soil.» | *«You'll weed my veg patch every Saturday for a month.»
